{"name":"Jocular Green","hex":"#cce2ca","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jocular","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Humorous, amusing or joking."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jocular"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Joke","blocks":[{"text":"A joke is a display of humour in which words are used within a specific and well-defined narrative structure to make people laugh and is usually not meant to be interpreted literally. It usually takes the form of a story, often with dialogue, and ends in a punch line, whereby the humorous element of the story is revealed; this can be done using a pun or other type of word play, irony or sarcasm, logical incompatibility, hyperbole, or other means. Linguist Robert Hetzron offers the definition:"},{"text":"A joke is a short humorous piece of oral literature in which the funniness culminates in the final sentence, called the punchline… In fact, the main condition is that the tension should reach its highest level at the very end. No continuation relieving the tension should be added. As for its being \"oral,\" it is true that jokes may appear printed, but when further transferred, there is no obligation to reproduce the text verbatim, as in the case of poetry."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Boris_Yeltsin_with_Bill_Clinton-1_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Boris_Yeltsin_with_Bill_Clinton-1_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joke"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/PapyrusWestcar_photomerge-AltesMuseum-Berlin-1A.jpg/500px-PapyrusWestcar_photomerge-AltesMuseum-Berlin-1A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/PapyrusWestcar_photomerge-AltesMuseum-Berlin-1A.jpg/1280px-PapyrusWestcar_photomerge-AltesMuseum-Berlin-1A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/PapyrusWestcar_photomerge-AltesMuseum-Berlin-1A.jpg/500px-PapyrusWestcar_photomerge-AltesMuseum-Berlin-1A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Westcar Papyrus, dating to c. 1600 BC, contains an example of one of the earliest surviving jokes.[5]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APapyrusWestcar_photomerge-AltesMuseum-Berlin-1A.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Why_did_the_chicken_cross_the_road%3F.JPG/500px-Why_did_the_chicken_cross_the_road%3F.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Why_did_the_chicken_cross_the_road%3F.JPG/500px-Why_did_the_chicken_cross_the_road%3F.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Why did the chicken cross the road? To get to the other side.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhy_did_the_chicken_cross_the_road%3F.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Sigmund_Freud%2C_by_Max_Halberstadt_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Sigmund_Freud%2C_by_Max_Halberstadt_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Sigmund_Freud%2C_by_Max_Halberstadt_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Sigmund_Freud%2C_by_Max_Halberstadt_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Sigmund_Freud%2C_by_Max_Halberstadt_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Sigmund_Freud%2C_by_Max_Halberstadt_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sigmund Freud","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASigmund_Freud%2C_by_Max_Halberstadt_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Charles_Darwin_by_Julia_Margaret_Cameron%2C_c._1868.jpg/500px-Charles_Darwin_by_Julia_Margaret_Cameron%2C_c._1868.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Charles_Darwin_by_Julia_Margaret_Cameron%2C_c._1868.jpg/1280px-Charles_Darwin_by_